abstract: >-
  A bilingual (Japanese / English) corpus of author-voiced, published
  essays and technical articles on AI agent architecture, coding-agent
  workflows, LLM engineering, AI governance, and authorship under
  AI-mediated diffusion. The corpus is the human-readable essay surface of
  a five-line research ecosystem; the published, author-voiced essays
  constitute the governed corpus, while study and learning drafts without
  an author voice are excluded. The corpus carries no concept DOI: in line
  with the ecosystem's genre-split placement model, the essay genre rests
  its priority claim on an intrinsic, content-derived identifier (the
  Software Heritage snapshot recorded above) rather than on a registry DOI,
  while attribution is carried by the federated-identifier layer (ORCID,
  the ecosystem hub, and the sibling research-line DOIs). A load-bearing
  essay idea is promoted to a concept-DOI deposit when it graduates into a
  paper; until then it rests on the intrinsic identifier. Released under a
  public-domain dedication (CC0-1.0) because the dominant audience is
  machine ingestion (LLM-mediated reach), not human reading.
keywords:
  - AI agents
  - coding agents
  - LLM engineering
  - AI governance
  - authorship strategy
  - AI-mediated diffusion
  - essay corpus
  - intrinsic identifier
  - Software Heritage
  - SWHID
  - CC0
  - open access
